  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 200 PM CDT Sat Sep 26 2026\n\nAn upper level blocking pattern continues across much of the\ncountry, with IL nestled between a deep coastal low \n(Nor'easter) tracking along the Mid Atlantic/New England \nseaboard through Monday and a shortwave trough lifting north/NE \nacross the Great Plains toward the Upper Mississippi Valley. \nMid level convergence and moisture advection will support \nisolated to scattered mainly light showers into this evening\nover west central IL, primarily along and west of a Galesburg to\nSpringfield line. Areas further east are drier with dewpoints\nof 45-50F from persistent gentle east to NE flow so this will \nkeep shower chances further west. The latest CAM runs keep\npatchy fog near or northeast of Kankakee and Lafayette IN \novernight and over nw Iowa. Lows overnight range from lower 50s\nin eastern IL (upper 40s possible from Champaign and Robinson\neast/ne and upper 50s in cloudier areas from Galesburg to\nSpringfield west/sw.  \n\nNice early fall weather should continue Sunday and Monday across\nthe area with persistent blocking pattern remaining with\nCanadian high pressure continuing to ridge toward IL with light\neast to NE winds. Western and sw CWA will continue to see more \nmid level clouds than eastern IL where partly to mostly sunny \nskies should prevail. Seasonable highs in the low to mid 70s \nwith lows Sunday night mostly lower 50s, but ranging from \naround 50 in eastern IL and mid 50s over west central IL/MS \nriver valley. \n\nThe Nor'easter to weaken and start pulling away from the New\nEngland Coast Monday night and Tue while high pressure ridge \nsettles into the Mid Atlantic Region giving a southerly flow of \na bit warmer air into IL. Highs Tue in the upper 70s to near 80F\nwith increasing clouds from the west during Tue afternoon ahead\nof an approaching frontal boundary in the Central Plains. It \nshould be dry during daytime hours over CWA on Tue with chances \nof showers and a few thunderstorms arriving over the IL river \nvalley Tue evening and over rest of central IL overnight Tue \nnight. Chances of showers and thunderstorms likely holds off \nuntil Wed in southeast IL. \n\nRepeated rounds of showers and a few thunderstorms appears\nlikely from overnight Tue night through Friday evening.\nVery high PWATs of 1.5-2.25 inches from Wed through Friday along\nwith remnants of Hurricane Polo interacting with the frontal\nboundary near IL Wed-Fri to support moderate to heavy rains at \ntimes. NBM has 30-50% chance over over 2 inches of rain over \nmuch of CWA during this time frame. Weak instability and wind \nshear parameters to keep the severe weather threat low with \nlocalized flooding the main concern. Latest extended models are\ntrending wetter during next weekend with another deep mid/upper\nlevel trof moving into the Midwest.",
